Count the no-newline marker as a marker, not as a line

libgit2 reports "no newline at end of file" as three origins of its own: `=`
when neither side has the trailing newline, `>` when the old side had it, `<`
when the new side gained it. The first pass folded `>` into `+` and `<` into
`-`, on the reading that they were the added and deleted final line.

They are not lines of content. A file whose last line lost its newline counted
two additions where git counts one, and drew the marker as a line of code. All
three are the marker row now, which is what git prints and what a patch rebuilt
from these rows has to emit.

Found while working out what hunk staging would need from these rows, which is
the same question. The text comes from here rather than from libgit2, whose
content for those origins is the line the marker follows.

